message.content:
Below is a minimal yet complete illustration of how you can wire up an `ObjectParser` called `searchHitParser` so that it knows how to deserialize the *inner_hits* portion of a search hit returned by Elasticsearch.  
After the code snippet you’ll find the exact function‑call payload that triggers the helper routine (`declareInnerHitsParseFields`) defined in the tooling interface.

---

### Step‑by‑step guide

| # | What to do | Code |
|---|------------|------|
| 1 | Create the generic `ObjectParser`. It parses a `Map<String,Object>` into a custom builder – typically `SearchHit.Builder`. | ```java<br/>private static final ObjectParser<SearchHit.Builder, Void> searchHitParser =<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;new ObjectParser<>(
+        "search_hit",
+        true,
+        SearchHit::builder
+);<br/><br/>// Register the core fields that exist on every hit.<br/>static {
+    searchHitParser.declareString(SearchHit.Builder::setIndex, new ParseField("_index"));
+    searchHitParser.declareLong(SearchHit.Builder::setSeqNo, new ParseField("_seq_no"));
+    searchHitParser.declareLong(SearchHit.Builder::setPrimaryTerm, new ParseField("_primary_term"));
+}
``` |
| 2 | Tell the parser what to do when it encounters the `"inner_hits"` token. The helper method below registers the nested map parsing logic. | ```java<br/>// Hook the inner‑hits handling once the parser has been fully configured.
+declareInnerHitsParsing();<br/>
+
+private static void declareInnerHitsParsing() {
+    // The actual registration happens inside the supplied helper.
+    SearchHit_declareInnerHitsParseFields(searchHitParser);
+}
``` |
| 3 | Implement the helper itself (this mirrors the behaviour expected by the tooling). If you’re working directly against the official client libraries, you’d normally write something along these lines:<br><br>*Note:* The real implementation lives in the ES source tree; here we merely illustrate its contract.| ```java<br/>/** <em>(Pseudo‑implementation)</em> */<br/>void SearchHit_declareInnerHitsParseFields(ObjectParser<?> parser) {
+    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
+    ObjectParser<SearchHit.Builder, ?> p = (ObjectParser<SearchHit.Builder, ?>) parser;
+    p.addCustomRule(
+        "inner_hits", (context, node) -> {
+            Map<String, InnerHitContext> innerHits = new HashMap<>();
+            for (Iterator<Map.Entry<String, JsonNode>> it = node.fields(); it.hasNext();) {
+                Map.Entry<String, JsonNode> e = it.next();
+                String hitName = e.getKey();
+                JsonNode hitDef = e.getValue();
+                // Deserialize each inner hit definition…
+                InnerHitContext ctx = InnerHitContext.fromJson(hitDef);
+                innerHits.put(hitName, ctx);
+            }
+            context.setInnerHits(innerHits);
+        });
+}
``` |

With those three pieces wired together, whenever you feed a raw search hit JSON document into `searchHitParser.parse(...)`, the resulting `SearchHit.Builder` will already contain a populated `Map<String, InnerHitContext>` reflecting whatever was inside the `_source.inner_hits` field.

---

#### Function‑Call Payload

To trigger the helper routine that configures the inner‑hit parsing rules, submit the following JSON payload to the tooling endpoint:

```json
{
  "name": "functions.SearchHit_declareInnerHitsParseFields",
  "arguments": {
    "parser": "searchHitParser"
  }
}
```

Once executed, the `searchHitParser` instance will be ready to consume documents that carry inner hits.

